# Extract Authoritative Knowledge

This is a bounded authority-extraction runtime call. Extract explicit, durable constraints from the supplied authoritative sources. Do not build a project map, inspect unrelated source code, infer implementation patterns, or rely on hidden memory from other stages.

Project:
- name: {{.ProjectName}}
- path: {{.RootPath}}

Authoritative knowledge paths are available at `{{.EngineeringKnowledgePath}}` ({{.EngineeringKnowledgeCount}} files). Read every listed file.
The immutable authority section catalog is available at `{{.AuthoritySectionsPath}}` ({{.AuthoritySectionCount}} sections). Each entry provides the only valid `section_id`, source, and section ownership.
{{- if .UserContextPath}}
User context is available at `{{.UserContextPath}}`. Treat it as authoritative only within the catalog entry assigned to user context; do not use it to rewrite or broaden another source.
{{- end}}

Extraction rules:
- Return exactly one `authority_sections` result for every catalog entry. Never invent, omit, duplicate, or rewrite a section ID.
- Extract only explicit instructions that constrain future changes. Keep advisory, conditional, local, and operation-specific wording at its stated strength and scope.
- Treat every explicit normative statement in every listed section as an independent extraction obligation. Do not omit a narrow rule because another rule appears more prominent.
- Do not turn source-code observations, common engineering practice, descriptive project facts, inferred architecture, or repeated implementation shape into authority rules.
- Do not promote a local source into a broader rule. Ownership and evidence are assigned by the runtime from `section_id`; do not return source, section, or evidence fields.
- Keep procedures owned by user-maintained workflows out of project knowledge. An explicit permission or prohibition governing whether a command may be executed is still an authority rule; a sequence of operational steps is not.
- Put concrete governed tasks, paths, commands, modules, interfaces, fields, or operations in `applies_to` when the source states them. Never guess missing scope.
- When a source explicitly governs command execution, set `command_policy` to exactly `forbidden`, `describe_only`, `requires_authorization`, or `allowed`. `forbidden` means the Agent must not execute it, including when the text says the user must perform it manually; `requires_authorization` applies only when the text explicitly permits execution after the user's current-turn approval; `describe_only` means explain but do not execute; `allowed` requires an explicit permission for the Agent to execute. Merely mentioning a command never implies `allowed`.
- Preserve every distinct constraint, but use the same concise title, scope, and command policy for repeated wording from the same source so the deterministic consolidation can keep one canonical projection.
- Describe what the source requires without strengthening it into a security, atomicity, correctness, reliability, or compatibility guarantee that the text does not make.
- When a section has no explicit constraint, return a concrete `no_rule_reason`. `rules` and `no_rule_reason` are mutually exclusive. Never use a placeholder.

Before returning, audit the section catalog for complete one-to-one coverage and audit each extracted statement against its owning source section.

Return JSON only.

